LS.add({"207624e2ed":{"t":"S29 Gabriel 加百列","b":1,"k":"unit","h":"<nav class=\"cr\" style=\"--c:#c0841a\"><a href=\"index.html\">燈臺 <i lang=\"en\">The Lampstand</i></a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl0\" href=\"#ic52d1cf364\">1 原文研究 Original Languages</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl1\" href=\"#ie883cc6886\">1 詞彙 Vocabulary</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl2\" href=\"#i52badd311e\">Studies 研究</a></nav><h1 class=\"fl3\" id=\"s29--加百列-gabriel\">S29 · <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> Gabriel</h1><details class=\"toc\"><summary>目錄 <i lang=\"en\">Contents</i></summary><ol><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\">1. At a glance 一覽</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#2-root-and-family-%E5%AD%97%E6%A0%B9%E8%88%87%E5%AD%97%E6%97%8F\">2. Root and family 字根與字族</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#3-counts-and-significance-%E6%95%B8%E6%93%9A%E8%88%87%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">3. Counts and significance 數據與意義</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#1-totals-%E7%B8%BD%E6%95%B8\">1. Totals 總數</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#2-by-book-%E6%8C%89%E6%9B%B8%E5%8D%B7\">2. By book 按書卷</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#3-by-section-%E6%8C%89%E5%88%86%E6%AE%B5\">3. By section 按分段</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#4-by-grammatical-form-%E6%8C%89%E8%A9%9E%E5%BD%A2\">4. By grammatical form 按詞形</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#5-by-translation-%E6%8C%89%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC\">5. By translation 按譯本</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-%E9%A6%96%E6%AC%A1%E8%88%87%E6%9C%AB%E6%AC%A1%E6%8E%92%E5%90%8D%E7%BD%95%E7%94%A8\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax 首次與末次、排名、罕用</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#7-spread-%E5%88%86%E4%BD%88\">7. Spread 分佈</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#8-significance-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">8. Significance 意義</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#4-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC-renderings-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E8%AD%AF%E6%B3%95-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. 和合本 renderings 和合本的譯法 (every rendering, every verse)</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#5-shades-of-meaning-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9%E5%B1%A4%E6%AC%A1\">5. Shades of meaning 意義層次</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#6-the-lxx-bridge-%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%81%E5%A3%AB%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E6%A9%8B%E6%A8%91\">6. The LXX bridge 七十士譯本的橋樑</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#7-companions-and-cross-references-%E5%90%8C%E4%BC%B4%E5%AD%97%E8%88%87%E4%BA%92%E5%8F%83\">7. Companions and cross-references 同伴字與互參</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#8-in-the-new-testament-%E6%96%B0%E7%B4%84%E7%9A%84%E7%94%A8%E6%B3%95\">8. In the New Testament 新約的用法</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#9-culture-and-background-%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E8%83%8C%E6%99%AF\">9. Culture and background 文化背景</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#10-theology-%E7%A5%9E%E5%AD%B8\">10. Theology 神學</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#12-for-practice-%E6%87%89%E7%94%A8\">11. For practice 應用</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#13-evaluation-%E8%A9%95%E4%BC%B0\">12. Evaluation 評估</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#14-sources-and-to-check-%E8%B3%87%E6%96%99%E8%88%87%E5%BE%85%E6%9F%A5\">13. Sources 資料</a></li></ol></details>\n<p><span class=\"off\">Angels <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Jesus Christ <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Scripture <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經論</span></span><span class=\"off\"></span></p>\n<h2 id=\"1-at-a-glance-一覽\">1. At a glance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一覽</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Hebrew:</strong> <big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span></big> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></p>\n<p><strong>Greek:</strong> <big><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span></big> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Four verses.</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> stands 2 times, in 2 verses, both in Daniel. <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> stands 2 times, in 2 verses, both in the first chapter of Luke. No other verse in the Bible names him. The study is short because the evidence is short: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">9:21</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">26</span>. The name is a proper noun with one spelling, so the forms and companions that fill the larger studies have little to report here.</li>\n<li><strong>In the Hebrew the name is two words joined.</strong> Strong's gives 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> H1397 and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵל</span> H410; man of God'. A verse before the name first appears, Daniel sees someone \"as the appearance of a man.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כְּ מַרְאֵה־ גָֽבֶר׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span>, BHSA)). So the noun <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> comes first, and the name built on it comes next. What the name means is debated (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>The Ulai vision: he is told to explain.</strong> Gabriel is named first in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span> (the first occurrence in canonical order), and the name is the vocative of a command: \"And I heard a man's voice between the banks of the Ulai, which called, and said, Gabriel, make this man to understand the vision.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵ֕ל הָבֵ֥ן לְ הַ לָּ֖ז אֶת־ הַ מַּרְאֶֽה׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, BHSA)). The verb <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">הָבֵן</span> is a Hiphil imperative of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בִּין</span>, cause to understand, which comes again in the next verse. Who speaks is not said.</li>\n<li><strong>During Daniel's prayer: the same one returns.</strong> \"yea, while I was speaking in prayer, the man Gabriel, whom I had seen in the vision at the beginning, being caused to fly swiftly, touched me about the time of the evening oblation.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ הָ אִ֣ישׁ גַּבְרִיאֵ֡ל אֲשֶׁר֩ רָאִ֨יתִי בֶ  חָזֹ֤ון בַּ  תְּחִלָּה֙</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, BHSA)). He calls his errand \"to give thee wisdom and understanding\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.22\">Dan 9:22</span>, ASV), and the seventy weeks follow (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.24-27\">Dan 9:24–27</span>). The words behind being caused to fly swiftly are disputed (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>In the temple: he names himself.</strong> \"I am Gabriel, that stand in the presence of God; and I was sent to speak unto thee, and to bring thee these good tidings.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, ASV) (<em><span lang=\"grc\">Ἐγώ εἰμι Γαβριὴλ ὁ παρεστηκὼς ἐνώπιον τοῦ Θεοῦ καὶ ἀπεστάλην λαλῆσαι πρὸς σὲ καὶ εὐαγγελίσασθαί σοι ταῦτα</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, OpenGNT)). Luke's narrative has already called him \"an angel of the Lord\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.11\">Luke 1:11</span>, ASV). Here he says where he stands, who sent him, and that the message is good news.</li>\n<li><strong>At Nazareth: sent to Mary.</strong> \"Now in the sixth month the angel Gabriel was sent from God unto a city of Galilee, named Nazareth,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>, ASV) (<em><span lang=\"grc\">Ἐν δὲ τῷ μηνὶ τῷ ἕκτῳ ἀπεστάλη ὁ ἄγγελος Γαβριὴλ ἀπὸ τοῦ Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>, OpenGNT)). He announces the conception of Jesus, \"He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Most High\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.32\">Luke 1:32</span>, ASV), and his last recorded words are \"For no word from God shall be void of power.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.37\">Luke 1:37</span>, ASV)</li>\n<li><strong>One task throughout.</strong> In all four verses he comes with understanding or with news. In the book of Daniel no battle is given to him by name, and in Luke he announces and answers questions. The name of Michael goes with contending and the name of Gabriel with explaining and announcing (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>What is not said.</strong> No verse calls Gabriel an archangel, or says how many such beings there are, or describes wings on him, or gives him a trumpet. A popular image has him blowing the last trumpet, but no verse says so. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Thess.4.16\">1 Thess 4:16</span> names a voice of an archangel and a trumpet of God and leaves the archangel unnamed. Whether the unnamed angel of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.1.20\">Matt 1:20</span> or <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.2.9\">Luke 2:9</span> is Gabriel is not said.</li>\n<li><strong>Where it stands (log-likelihood):</strong> Daniel 2 of the 2 (G² 15.9), Luke 2 of the 2 (G² 7.8). By section: the Major Prophets 2, the Gospels 2. With two occurrences on each side these figures describe where the name stands and little more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">看圖</span> See it</strong> — the counts by book and by section are the two charts of section 3; the renderings with their counts are sections 3.7 and 4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bible circles <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經圓圖</span>: <span class=\"off\">Daniel 8, the Ulai</span> · <span class=\"off\">Daniel 9, the prayer and the seventy weeks</span> · <span class=\"off\">Luke 1, the temple and Nazareth</span></li>\n<li>People (the circles' people, four languages): <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">但以理</span> Daniel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">דָּנִיֵּאל</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Δανιήλ</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒迦利亞</span> Zechariah <span lang=\"grc\">Ζαχαρίας</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以利沙伯</span> Elizabeth <span lang=\"grc\">Ἐλισάβετ</span></span> (Luke 1) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">馬利亞</span> Mary <span lang=\"grc\">Μαρία</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.27\">Luke 1:27</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">約瑟</span> Joseph <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰωσήφ</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.27\">Luke 1:27</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">約翰</span> John <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰωάννης</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.13\">Luke 1:13</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">耶穌</span> Jesus <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰησοῦς</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.31\">Luke 1:31</span>).</li>\n<li>Places: <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">烏萊</span> Ulai <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אוּלַי</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">書珊</span> Susa <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שׁוּשַׁן</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.2\">Dan 8:2</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">拿撒勒</span> Nazareth <span lang=\"grc\">Ναζαρέθ</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加利利</span> Galilee <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גָּלִיל</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Γαλιλαία</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>) · <span class=\"off\">the Holy Land in 3D <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖地立體地圖</span></span> (the map has no place links yet: find them by name)</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">我又聽見烏萊河兩岸中有人聲呼叫說：「加百列啊，要使此人明白這異象。」（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">但以理書 8:16</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">And I heard a man's voice between the banks of the Ulai, which called, and said, Gabriel, make this man to understand the vision. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Daniel 8:16</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">我正禱告的時候，先前在異象中所見的那位加百列，奉命迅速飛來，約在獻晚祭的時候，按手在我身上。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">但以理書 9:21</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">yea, while I was speaking in prayer, the man Gabriel, whom I had seen in the vision at the beginning, being caused to fly swiftly, touched me about the time of the evening oblation. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Daniel 9:21</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使回答說：「我是站在神面前的加百列，奉差而來對你說話，將這好信息報給你。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">路加福音 1:19</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">And the angel answering said unto him, I am Gabriel, that stand in the presence of God; and I was sent to speak unto thee, and to bring thee these good tidings. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">到了第六個月，天使加百列奉神的差遣往加利利的一座城去（這城名叫拿撒勒），（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">路加福音 1:26</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">Now in the sixth month the angel Gabriel was sent from God unto a city of Galilee, named Nazareth, (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"2-root-and-family-字根與字族\">2. Root and family <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">字根與字族</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Greek words of this study taken from Hebrew (Strong's: of Hebrew origin):</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span> from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span> (of Hebrew origin).</p>\n<p><strong>Compounds in this study (Strong's data):</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span> = <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> <em>geber</em> <span class=\"off\">H1397</span> + <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵל</span> <em>ʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H410</span>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> (H1403): Strong's 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> H1397 and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵל</span> H410; man of God'. BDB glosses the name 'man of El' and calls him 'an arch-angel', and gives <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span> with a comparison to <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>. UBS: 'a heavenly creature'. Both occurrences are the same form in BHSA: a proper noun, masculine, singular, absolute. It has no article and no plural.</li>\n<li>The two parts. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> (H1397, 65 times) is from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גָּבַר</span> (H1396, 25 times), be strong; BDB explains the noun as 'man as strong, distinguished from women, children, and non-combatants whom he is to defend, chiefly poetic'. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גִּבֹּור</span> (H1368, 159 times) is from the same root, and BDB calls it an 'attribute of God especially as fighting for his people'. The Aramaic man is <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גְּבַר</span> (H1400, 21 times), which Daniel also uses. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵל</span> (H410, 248 times) is God. The same two roots, in reversed order, stand in the title \"Mighty God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.9.6\">Isa 9:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵ֣ל גִּבֹּ֔ור</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.9.6\">Isa 9:6</span>, BHSA)).</li>\n<li>Four men in four verses. Three different Hebrew words for man stand in the verses around his name, and the name is built on one of them: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span>, \"as the appearance of a man.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span>, ASV); <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אָדָם</span>, \"a man's voice\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קֹול־ אָדָ֖ם</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, BHSA)); <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֶּן־אָדָם</span>, Daniel himself, \"Understand, O son of man\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.17\">Dan 8:17</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">הָבֵ֣ן בֶּן־ אָדָ֔ם</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.17\">Dan 8:17</span>, BHSA)); and at Dan 9:21 a third word, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אִישׁ</span>, in <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וְ הָ אִ֣ישׁ גַּבְרִיאֵ֡ל</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, BHSA). Keil says the address son of man 'is designed to remind Daniel of his human weakness' and sets it in contrast to Gabriel's name.</li>\n<li><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> (G1043): Strong's 'of Hebrew origin (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> H1403)', 'Gabriel, an archangel'. Abbott-Smith has 'hero of God' for the Hebrew and 'the archangel Gabriel'; UBS 'an archangel'. It is indeclinable. Both occurrences in OpenGNT are nominative, and both are singular.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-counts-and-significance-數據與意義\">3. Counts and significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">數據與意義</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Sources: OT = BHSA (ETCBC words, prefixes count as words); NT = OpenGNT 3.3; KJV and ASV = eBible texts with Strong's tags; <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> = the word-by-word alignments (OpenHebrewBible, OpenGNT). Tests: log-likelihood G² with 1 degree of freedom.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"1-totals-總數\">1. Totals <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">總數</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>Testament</th><th>Words</th><th>Verses</th><th>Forms</th><th>Commonest forms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></td><td>OT</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵ֕ל</span> 1, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵ֡ל</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> family</strong></td><td>OT</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>2</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></td><td>NT</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριὴλ</span> 2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> family</strong></td><td>NT</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>1</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Whole Bible:</strong> 2 Hebrew words + 2 Greek words = 4 (the two languages are counted in their own texts; the Septuagint is not added).</p>\n<p><strong>Lexeme check (Strong's tag against the BHSA lexeme):</strong> every word counted carries the same BHSA lexeme as its Strong's number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-by-book-按書卷\">2. By book <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按書卷</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S29 Gabriel - books.svg\" src=\"pages/8d112636c1.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span>.</strong> Most uses: Dan 2. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Dan 2.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> Dan (G² 16, p &lt; 0.0001, expected under 0.05).</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dan <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">但以理書</span></td><td>2</td><td>2.48</td><td>0.0</td><td>15.9</td><td>p &lt; 0.0001</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span>.</strong> Most uses: Luke 2. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Luke 1.0.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> no book stands out at p &lt; 0.001.</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Luke <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">路加福音</span></td><td>2</td><td>1.03</td><td>0.3</td><td>7.8</td><td>p &lt; 0.01</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><em>G² (log-likelihood) compares the count in a book with what the book's size predicts; G² ≥ 10.83 means p &lt; 0.001, a chance below 1 in 1,000 that the excess is luck.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"3-by-section-按分段\">3. By section <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按分段</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S29 Gabriel - sections.svg\" src=\"pages/0732d9a418.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.5</td><td>1.2</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>History <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.7</td><td>1.8</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Wisdom and Poetry <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">詩歌智慧書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.5</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Major Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span></td><td>2</td><td>0.23</td><td>0.4</td><td>6.3</td><td>p &lt; 0.05</td></tr><tr><td>Minor Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">小先知書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.1</td><td>0.2</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gospels <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">福音書</span></td><td>2</td><td>0.31</td><td>0.9</td><td>3.0</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Acts <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使徒行傳</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.6</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Paul's letters <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅書信</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.5</td><td>1.1</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Hebrews and the General letters <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">希伯來書與普通書信</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.4</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Revelation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">啟示錄</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.1</td><td>0.3</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"4-by-grammatical-form-按詞形\">4. By grammatical form <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按詞形</span></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> proper noun 2 · <em>number:</em> singular 2 · <em>state:</em> absolute 2 · <em>pronoun suffix:</em> none 2</li>\n<li><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> noun 2 · <em>case:</em> nominative 2 · <em>number:</em> singular 2</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"5-by-translation-按譯本\">5. By translation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按譯本</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>KJV (its own tags)</th><th>ASV (machine-tagged)</th><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> (alignment; full list in section 4)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></td><td>Gabriel 2</td><td>Gabriel 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> 2</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></td><td>Gabriel 2</td><td>Gabriel 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>The KJV counts are its own Strong's tags. The ASV's tags were added by machine and sometimes sit on the wrong word, so its counts are a guide only; \"–\" = no tag found. Small words that carry a tag (and, of, the, for, let …) are left out of both lists.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-首次與末次排名罕用\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">首次與末次、排名、罕用</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>First</th><th>Last</th><th>Rank among all lemmas</th><th>Once only?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span></td><td>4734 of 8,563 (OT)</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span></td><td>2652 of 5,399 (NT)</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"7-spread-分佈\">7. Spread <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">分佈</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma / family</th><th>Books with it</th><th>DP (0 = even, 1 = one corner)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></td><td>1 of 39</td><td>0.981</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> family</strong></td><td>1 of 39</td><td><strong>0.981</strong></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></td><td>1 of 27</td><td>0.859</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> family</strong></td><td>1 of 27</td><td><strong>0.859</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>DP (Gries' deviation of proportions): how far the word's spread over the books is from what the book sizes predict.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"8-significance-意義\">8. Significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義</span></h3>\n<p><strong>Statistical (log-likelihood G², chi-square with 1 df):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: none at p &lt; 0.001; books: Dan (G² 16).</li>\n<li><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: none at p &lt; 0.001; books: none at p &lt; 0.001.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In context (a judgment — the rubric of the template: F first mention · P programmatic or climactic · C covenant formula or God describing himself · Q quoted in the NT or quoting the OT · A carries the argument):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Passage</th><th>F</th><th>P</th><th>C</th><th>Q</th><th>A</th><th>Rating</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td><td>F</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>first occurrence in canonical order: \"Gabriel, make this man to understand the vision.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>he comes during prayer, and the seventy weeks follow: \"the man Gabriel, whom I had seen in the vision at the beginning\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span></td><td>F</td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★★</td><td>first in the New Testament, in the first person: \"I am Gabriel, that stand in the presence of God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>the annunciation to Mary; last use: \"the angel Gabriel was sent from God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>, ASV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Data behind \"A\" — where the words are densest (ten-verse stretches with three or more uses):</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span>: no dense stretch.</li>\n<li>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span>: no dense stretch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-和合本-renderings-和合本的譯法-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> renderings <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本的譯法</span> (every rendering, every verse) <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>From the word-by-word alignments (OT: OpenHebrewBible CUV-to-BHS; NT: OpenGNT CUVtc). Pronouns and particles at the edges are set aside for grouping (<span lang=\"zh-Hant\">他的慈愛</span> is listed under <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">慈愛</span>, and the raw forms are kept). A use the alignment does not tie to a Chinese word is listed as \"not tied\".</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"גבריאל-gabrîyʼêl-h1403--2-uses-1-renderings\"><big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span></big> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span> — 2 uses, 1 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span></strong></td><td>2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span> 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (H1403)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span></strong> (2): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">9:21</span></p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"γαβριήλ-gabriḗl-g1043--2-uses-1-renderings\"><big><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span></big> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span> — 2 uses, 1 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span></strong></td><td>2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">福音書</span> 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (G1043)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span></strong> (2): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">1:26</span></p></div></details>\n<p><strong>What the variety shows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The counting engine aligns <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> to 2 of the 2 Hebrew occurrences and to 2 of the 2 Greek ones. Counted by string, <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> stands in 4 verses of the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span>, the same four as the ASV's Gabriel. The name is transliterated and does not carry its meaning, so a reader of the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> meets a sound and no word for man.</li>\n<li>The man in the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span>. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span> it has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「忽有一位形狀像人的站在我面前」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, and at Dan 8:16 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「有人聲呼叫」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, where the Hebrew has the article and the noun man, it has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「那位加百列」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, the ASV \"the man Gabriel\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ASV), and the Greek used here <em><span lang=\"grc\">ανήρ Γαβριήλ</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ABP). So the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> keeps the identification with the one seen earlier and leaves out the word.</li>\n<li>The disputed participle. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「奉命迅速飛來，約在獻晚祭的時候，按手在我身上」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. The <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> chooses flying, as the ASV and KJV do, and adds <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">奉命</span>, by command, for the passive sense of the participle. In the same verse the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> reads the verb as a touch of the hand, <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按手</span>, where the Hebrew has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נֹגֵ֣עַ אֵלַ֔י</span>. Section 10 sets out why the participle <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מֻעָף</span> is disputed.</li>\n<li>Titles. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使長</span>, archangel, is not used of him anywhere in the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span>: by string that title stands in four verses, none of which names Gabriel (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27</a>, section 4). At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span> the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「天使加百列奉神的差遣」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>.</li>\n<li>The word to Mary. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「蒙大恩的女子，我問你安，主和你同在了！」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.28\">Luke 1:28</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span> The ASV has \"Hail, thou that art highly favored, the Lord is with thee.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.28\">Luke 1:28</span>, ASV) The KJV adds \"blessed art thou among women.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.28\">Luke 1:28</span>, KJV), following the Textus Receptus. The critical text followed by OpenGNT lacks it there, and it stands in Elisabeth's mouth at \"Blessed art thou among women\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.42\">Luke 1:42</span>, ASV).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-shades-of-meaning-意義層次\">5. Shades of meaning <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義層次</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>UBS Dictionary of Biblical Hebrew (SDBH 0.9.2) and UBS Greek NT Dictionary 1.1: each sense with its domain and its verses (Hebrew verse numbers mapped to the English ones).</em></p>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Domain</th><th>Definition</th><th>Verses</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gabriel</td><td>Names of Supernatural Beings</td><td>= a heavenly creature, ► who appeared to Daniel twice to pass on divine messages and help him understand the visions he had seen</td><td>2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Domain</th><th>Definition</th><th>Verses</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gabriel</td><td>Names of Persons and Places / Persons</td><td>an archangel</td><td>2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-lxx-bridge-七十士譯本的橋樑\">6. The LXX bridge <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">七十士譯本的橋樑</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Verse by verse: each Hebrew verse beside its Septuagint verse. <strong>Matched</strong> = verses where the Hebrew and the Greek word are paired one to one, the strongest pair first, so a Greek word already explained by another Hebrew word in the verse is not counted again: the probable rendering, to read in the verses. <strong>Together</strong> = verses where both merely stand. <strong>× chance</strong> = together ÷ what the two verse counts predict. A row with Together but Matched 0 is a companion in the verse, not a rendering.</em></p>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span> → Greek</strong> (of 2 verses with a Septuagint verse)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek in the LXX</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Matched</th><th>Together</th><th>× chance</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span></td><td>Gabriel</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>11624.5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">ὅρασις</span> <em>hórasis</em> <span class=\"off\">G3706</span></td><td>appearance/vision</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>261.2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">ἀνήρ</span> <em>anḗr</em> <span class=\"off\">G435</span></td><td>man</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>19.1</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> <em>Gabriḗl</em> <span class=\"off\">G1043</span> ← the Hebrew behind it in the LXX</strong> (of 2 verses)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hebrew</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Matched</th><th>Together</th><th>× chance</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> <em>Gabrîyʼêl</em> <span class=\"off\">H1403</span></td><td>Gabriel</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>11624.5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>OT verses with these Hebrew words that the NT quotes</strong> (chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New, verse by verse: a list, or OpenBible where the tagged words have no list address, names the link, the NT words are tagged as an OT quotation (Levinsohn), and the two verses share words): none listed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The LXX used here (Apostolic Bible Polyglot text, the Sixtine of 1587 by way of Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart; its Daniel is Theodotion) has <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> in 2 of the 2 verses, and in no others. <em><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ συνέτισον εκείνον την ορασιν</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, ABP) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">ανήρ Γαβριήλ ον είδον εν τη οράσει εν τη αρχή πετόμενος</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ABP). So the Greek of Luke keeps the name exactly as it stands in the Greek of Daniel.</li>\n<li>The Greek at Dan 9:21 settles nothing about the Hebrew. Theodotion has flying, <em><span lang=\"grc\">πετόμενος</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ABP), and the Greek has no word for weariness. Keil reports the older Greek as a rendering of swift motion and the Vulgate as 'cito volans', and he explains these renderings as a confusion of two verbs (section 10).</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>, for the Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כִּ֥י חֲמוּדֹ֖ות אָ֑תָּה</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>, BHSA), the Greek used here has <em><span lang=\"grc\">ότι ανήρ επιθυμιών ει συ</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>, ABP), a man of desires: this is Gabriel's word to Daniel. The same Greek phrase stands at Dan 10:11 for <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אִישׁ־ חֲ֠מֻדֹות</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.10.11\">Dan 10:11</span>, BHSA), where the speaker is not named.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-companions-and-cross-references-同伴字與互參\">7. Companions and cross-references <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">同伴字與互參</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Words that stand with it far more often than chance. Same verse, and within two verses either side (same book). Ranked by G²; PMI = log₂(observed ÷ expected): 1 = twice as often as chance, 3 = eight times.</em></p>\n\n<p><strong>Hebrew Gabriel <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span>, within two verses:</strong> .</p>\n\n<p><strong>Greek Gabriel <span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span>, within two verses:</strong> .</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Luke does not quote Daniel, but the two scenes share a pattern. Gabriel comes at an hour of worship in both: \"about the time of the evening oblation\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, ASV) and \"at the hour of incense.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.10\">Luke 1:10</span>, ASV) Prayer has been heard: \"At the beginning of thy supplications the commandment went forth\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>, ASV) and \"because thy supplication is heard\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.13\">Luke 1:13</span>, ASV). Fear falls on the one who sees him: \"I was affrighted, and fell upon my face\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.17\">Dan 8:17</span>, ASV) and \"fear fell upon him.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.12\">Luke 1:12</span>, ASV) Then silence: in a later vision where the speaker is unnamed, \"I set my face toward the ground, and was dumb.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.10.15\">Dan 10:15</span>, ASV), and in the temple, \"thou shalt be silent and not able to speak\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.20\">Luke 1:20</span>, ASV). That Luke meant the reader to think of Daniel is an inference. It is a strong one, because no other verse in the Bible names this angel.</li>\n<li>The content in Daniel and Luke. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.25\">Dan 9:25</span> speaks of \"unto the anointed one, the prince,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.25\">Dan 9:25</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עַד־ מָשִׁ֣יחַ נָגִ֔יד</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.25\">Dan 9:25</span>, BHSA)), and in Luke he announces the one to whom God gives \"the throne of his father David\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.32\">Luke 1:32</span>, ASV). Bengel says of Luke's Gabriel, 'It was the same angel, and he came on the same business.' He took that business to be 'the incarnation of the Son of God'. The identification of the anointed one in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.25\">Dan 9:25</span> is itself debated (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i1b5f27bacc\">S18 Messiah</a>).</li>\n<li>The title angel of the Lord. In Luke 1 one being is called \"an angel of the Lord\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.11\">Luke 1:11</span>, ASV), then \"the angel\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.13\">Luke 1:13</span>, ASV), and then at Luke 1:19 he names himself. In this chapter the title does not name a unique person: it describes someone who can be identified. The wider debate on the angel of the LORD is in <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\">S24 angel</a>.</li>\n<li>Standing before God. \"that stand in the presence of God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, ASV) uses the perfect participle of <span lang=\"grc\">παρίστημι</span>, and the seven of the Apocalypse are also said to stand before God: \"the seven angels that stand before God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.8.2\">Rev 8:2</span>, ASV). Bengel writes 'Seven angels stand in the presence of God' and puts Gabriel among them. Alford calls him 'one of the chief angels near the throne of God' and cites Tobit. Charles prints Tobit's words of Raphael as 'I am Raphael, one of the seven holy angels, which present the prayers of the saints, and go in before the glory of the Holy One'. The Bible gives Gabriel no number; those figures belong to a later tradition.</li>\n<li>Verse cross-references: \"Old in New\" is used in section 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"8-in-the-new-testament-新約的用法\">8. In the New Testament <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">新約的用法</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p>By section (from 3.3): Gospels 2, Acts 0, Paul's letters 0, Hebrews and the General letters 0, Revelation 0.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>.</strong> <em><span lang=\"grc\">ἀπεστάλην λαλῆσαι πρὸς σὲ καὶ εὐαγγελίσασθαί σοι ταῦτα</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, OpenGNT). The verb <span lang=\"grc\">εὐαγγελίζω</span> stands 54 times in the New Testament, ten of them in Luke's Gospel, and this is the first in that Gospel, so Bengel writes 'Thus marking the beginning of the Gospel'. The verb's link with <span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελος</span> and the counts of the verb and its noun are in <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\">S24</a>, sections 1 and 3.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26-38\">Luke 1:26–38</span>.</strong> <em><span lang=\"grc\">ἀπεστάλη ὁ ἄγγελος Γαβριὴλ ἀπὸ τοῦ Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>, OpenGNT). The sender is named, and the verb of sending is the one Hebrews uses of all angels. Someone always stands behind him: a voice at the Ulai (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>), a commandment that went forth (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>), and God (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">Luke 1:26</span>). His message is about Jesus: \"thou shalt conceive in thy womb, and bring forth a son, and shalt call his name JESUS.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.31\">Luke 1:31</span>, ASV) and \"The Holy Spirit shall come upon thee, and the power of the Most High shall overshadow thee\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.35\">Luke 1:35</span>, ASV).</li>\n<li><strong><span lang=\"grc\">κεχαριτωμένη</span>.</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">Χαῖρε κεχαριτωμένη</span>. The verb <span lang=\"grc\">χαριτόω</span> stands 2 times: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.28\">Luke 1:28</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Eph.1.6\">Eph 1:6</span>, where it is used of all the redeemed. Abbott-Smith gives 'to endue with grace (i.e. divine favour)' for both verses (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>Gabriel and unbelief.</strong> Zacharias asks for a sign: \"Whereby shall I know this?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.18\">Luke 1:18</span>, ASV) Mary asks a question too: \"How shall this be, seeing I know not a man?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.34\">Luke 1:34</span>, ASV) Zacharias is told he will be silent, \"because thou believedst not my words,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.20\">Luke 1:20</span>, ASV), and she is not. The text does not explain the difference beyond that phrase. Bengel writes that the question 'which Mary started as a question, was accompanied with faith'. Alford, on Zacharias, writes 'It is impossible for us to say what the degree of unbelief in Zacharias was'.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-culture-and-background-文化背景\">9. Culture and background <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文化背景</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Between the Testaments. 1 Enoch names him with other chief angels: 'And then Michael, Uriel, Raphael, and Gabriel looked down from heaven' (chapter 9). In chapter 10 he is sent against the giants: 'And to Gabriel said the Lord'. Chapter 20 lists him among the holy angels as the one 'who is over Paradise and the serpents and the Cherubim', and chapter 40, in the Parables, says that the one 'who is set over all the powers, is Gabriel' (R. H. Charles's translation, 1913). So in that literature he has a province. Dan 8–9 and Luke 1 give him no province, only errands. Tobit's seven are mentioned above (section 7).</li>\n<li>At Qumran. The ETCBC edition (Abegg, Bowley, Cook) has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גבריאל</span> eleven times, five in Hebrew and six in Aramaic. Graded by the edition's own letter marks, four are preserved, three partly preserved and four wholly in the editors' restorations. Preserved: 1QM 9:16 (the War Scroll's towers, with Michael first and Gabriel second); 1Q19bis f2:4, where the names before it (Michael, Uriel and part of Raphael) are the editors' restoration; and, in Aramaic, 4Q529 f1:4 and 4Q557 f1:2, where he is called the angel (that word partly restored in 4Q557). Partly preserved: 4Q285 f1:3 (Hebrew) and 4Q201 f1iv:6 and 4Q202 f1iii:7 (Aramaic). Wholly restored: 1QM 9:15, the first list of the towers; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span> itself (4Q113, fragment 18); 4Q202 f1iv:5, a copy of Enoch, at the line where the Lord speaks to Gabriel; and XQ8 f1:1, in a list with Michael, Sariel and Raphael.</li>\n<li>In the Targums. J. W. Etheridge's 1862 translation of the Palestinian Targum says at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.37.15\">Gen 37:15</span>, 'And Gabriel, in the likeness of a man, found him wandering in the field.', which supplies a name where Genesis has only a man. On <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.34.6\">Deut 34:6</span> see <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27</a>, section 9. The dating of these Targums is debated.</li>\n<li>In the Qur'an. Rodwell's 1861 version has 'Whoso is the enemy of Gabriel' and 'caused the Koran to descend on thy heart' in Sura 2 (Rodwell's Sura II, verse numbers differ from the standard numbering), and Rodwell's note says 'The speaker in this, as in all the Suras, is Gabriel'. In Islam he is the angel of revelation. In the Bible he never brings a book.</li>\n<li>Worship and art: the feast of the Annunciation on 25 March, the Ave Maria drawn from <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.28\">Luke 1:28</span> and 1:42, and the Orthodox and Roman feasts of the archangels. None of these is a word study, and they appear here because readers meet them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"10-theology-神學\">10. Theology <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神學</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What does the name mean? The debated point.</strong> Strong's, BDB, ISBE, Keil and Alford all give man of God or man of El. Abbott-Smith gives 'hero of God'. Rodwell, in a note to the Koran, gives 'the strong one of God'. Bengel takes it another way, saying the name 'indicates the main object of his embassy', and reads it as God is man, a statement about the incarnation. Keil, with Hengstenberg, Hofmann and Kliefoth, says 'Gabriel is not a nomen proprium but appellativum': the one who looked like a man is given this name so that Daniel may recognise him again. Modern reference works often give strong man of God or God is my warrior, depending on whether the yod is taken as a linking vowel or as my. No verse explains the name; the nearest to an explanation is <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span> in the verse before. So the meaning should not be pressed for a doctrine.</li>\n<li><strong>Is he an archangel? The debated point.</strong> The lexicons say so: BDB 'an arch-angel', Strong's and UBS 'an archangel', Abbott-Smith 'the archangel Gabriel'. ISBE objects: 'Though commonly spoken of as an archangel, he is not so called in Scripture.' Keil: 'As to his relation to other angels and archangels, the Scripture gives no information.' The texts agree with ISBE and Keil. The word <span lang=\"grc\">ἀρχάγγελος</span> stands twice in the New Testament, and neither verse names him (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27</a>). The title looks like a later inference from his standing before God and from 1 Enoch's lists.</li>\n<li><strong>Being caused to fly swiftly: the debated phrase.</strong> The Hebrew is <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מֻעָ֣ף בִּ יעָ֔ף</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span>, BHSA), and BHSA parses the first word as a Hophal participle of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יָעַף</span>, be weary (H3286), and the second as the noun <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יְעָף</span>, weariness (H3288). BDB gives 'weary with weariness' and adds that it is said of Gabriel, 'from winged flight'. Keil takes it another way. He joins the words to Daniel ('I had seen'), writes 'wearied in weariness, i.e. very wearied or tired', and says the words 'perfectly agree with the condition of Daniel'. He says the rendering by flight 'is without any foundation in the words', was 'probably derived by the old translators from a confounding of' this verb with the verb to fly (the Hebrew letters are damaged in the local OCR), and names Hävernick, von Lengerke and Hitzig, with the rabbi Jos. Jacchiades, as holding it. The ASV, KJV, the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> and Theodotion read flying, which would come from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עוּף</span> (H5774), a verb that does not stand in Daniel in BHSA. Strong's lists 'cause to fly' among the KJV's renderings of H3286 and 'swiftly' for H3288, which is the KJV's choice recorded. Keil notes that from these renderings 'the church fathers concluded that the angels were winged'. The two verbs give nearly the same form here, so the pointing alone does not end the debate. So: BHSA, BDB and Keil read weary; whether of Gabriel or of Daniel is debated; and the wings the Bible describes on heavenly beings stand on cherubim, seraphim and the living creatures (<a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i86d7c9abae\">S25</a>, <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#idac416b214\">S26</a>), not on Gabriel. Most modern translators keep the sense of flight.</li>\n<li><strong>Where did the angels' names come from? The debated point.</strong> Alford reports, 'The names of the angels, say the Rabbis, came up with Israel from Babylon', denies against Strauss and the rationalists that they were borrowed from a heathen system, and says 'their names formed matter of subsequent revelation to Daniel'. Keil's footnote denies that 'the doctrine of angels' or the names came from Persian thought. O. H. Gates in Hastings' <em>Dictionary of Christ and the Gospels</em> (1906) holds the opposite: 'Gabriel belongs to the creations of the imagination of the Jews in post-exilic times.' The local sources do not settle this. The dating of Daniel is part of the question: Keil defends the exilic setting, and most modern critical scholarship places the final form in the second century BC. Since the date of the book is contested, this study says only that <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span> is the first occurrence in canonical order. Whether the name in Daniel or the name in the Book of the Watchers (1 Enoch 1–36) stands earlier in date depends on those judgments, and this study does not decide that.</li>\n<li><strong>Who speaks at the Ulai? The debated point.</strong> Keil calls the one seen in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15\">Dan 8:15</span> 'a supernatural being in human likeness'. He connects the voice from between the banks, as a conjecture, with the man above the waters of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.12.6-7\">Dan 12:6–7</span>, who 'shows himself to be a divine being' and is described at Dan 10:5. The text leaves the voice unidentified. Who stands behind the speaker of Dan 10 is taken up in <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27</a>, section 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Greeting and grace: the debated word.</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">κεχαριτωμένη</span> is the perfect passive participle of the verb in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Eph.1.6\">Eph 1:6</span>, and it means one on whom favour has been shown. The Vulgate's gratia plena, full of grace, has been read in Catholic theology as a statement about Mary's state, and Reformation commentators read the participle as a receiving. What the word itself says is that she received favour, and Gabriel's explanation in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.30\">Luke 1:30</span> is \"for thou hast found favor with God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.30\">Luke 1:30</span>, ASV) The word says what God has done. It leaves open how far the title extends.</li>\n<li><strong>Messengers do not bring their own message.</strong> Gabriel says \"and I was sent\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, ASV). At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span> the word goes out before he comes. He does not invent the news, and his authority is the sender's. Hebrews draws the conclusion for all angels. O. H. Gates (in Hastings) says the Gospel mentions him 'as a messenger of the signal favour of God', and the rest of Luke 1 says what the favour is.</li>\n<li>Where the traditions differ (one neutral line): Roman Catholic and Orthodox Christians honour Gabriel with feasts and the Ave Maria; Protestants generally do not; Latter-day Saints identify Gabriel with Noah. All these traditions place Christ above every angel (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.4\">Heb 1:4</span>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"12-for-practice-應用\">11. For practice <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">應用</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teaching slide: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span> = <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span>; a man (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גֶּבֶר</span>) and God (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵל</span>); four verses, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.19\">Luke 1:19</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26\">26</span>; twice to make understand, twice to announce.</li>\n<li>Bible class: read <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15-19\">Dan 8:15–19</span> and 9:20–23, then <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.5-20\">Luke 1:5–20</span> and 1:26–38 side by side. Note the hour, the prayer, the fear, the fear not, the silence, and the message. Ask what the messenger adds to the message, and how Zacharias's question differs from Mary's.</li>\n<li>Personal: \"At the beginning of thy supplications the commandment went forth\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">Dan 9:23</span>, ASV). The answer began before the prayer ended, and the one who brought the answer stood before God. The same God receives prayer now.</li>\n<li>For preachers: keep Gabriel where the text puts him. Say what he explains and what he announces, and let the weight fall on the sender and on the Son he announces. The material on his rank belongs to Enoch and the later tradition, and it should be identified as such.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">連在一起</span> Connected</strong> — how they relate · why it matters · how we live it out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bible truths <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經真理</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">95 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的使者</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.26-38\">Luke 1:26–38</span> is its key passage) · <span class=\"off\">93 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使是受造的</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">94 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">服役的靈</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.14\">Heb 1:14</span>) · <span class=\"off\">96 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督超乎天使</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">05 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">道成肉身</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.31-35\">Luke 1:31–35</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>Book studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">書卷研究</span>:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i7a592efe93\">Hebrews</a> (1:14) · messengers and the Son · <span class=\"off\">Daniel</span> (8:16; 9:21) · <span class=\"off\">Luke</span> (1:19, 26).</li>\n<li><strong>World religions <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">世界宗教</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">Islam</span> · the angel of revelation (section 9).</li>\n<li><strong>World Watch <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">世界觀察</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">the Dead Sea Scrolls</span> · Gabriel in the War Scroll and the Aramaic Enoch (section 9).</li>\n<li><strong>Hymns <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖詩</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">Hark! the Herald Angels Sing <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聽阿天使高聲唱</span></span> (the angel's news) · <strong>Messiah:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#iadd9414a67\">M08 Behold, a virgin shall conceive</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.31\">Luke 1:31</span> echoes <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.7.14\">Isa 7:14</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>The Lampstand 燈臺: </strong>knop 1, the Word of God · an angel explains, and God sends.</li>\n<li><strong>Word studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">原文研究</span>:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\">S24 angel</a> (the larger family; <span lang=\"grc\">εὐαγγελίζω</span>; Heb 1) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27 Michael</a> (the other named angel) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i86d7c9abae\">S25 cherubim</a> and <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#idac416b214\">S26 seraphim</a> (the wings) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i1b5f27bacc\">S18 Messiah</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.25\">Dan 9:25</span>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">標籤詞</span> Tag words</strong> — <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">中文</span> · English · Hebrew · Greek (the shared vocabulary; counts = words in the text; a Hebrew or Greek word opens its word study, or its lexicon entry where no study exists yet):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>#t/gabriel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加百列</span> · Gabriel · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i207624e2ed\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">גַּבְרִיאֵל</span></a> H1403 (2) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i207624e2ed\"><span lang=\"grc\">Γαβριήλ</span></a> G1043 (2)</li>\n<li>#t/angel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使</span> · angel · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מַלְאָךְ</span></a> H4397 (213) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελος</span></a> G32 (175)</li>\n<li>#t/prayer <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">禱告</span> · prayer · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">תְּפִלָּה</span></span> H8605 (77) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">προσευχή</span></span> G4335 (36)</li>\n<li>#t/gospel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">福音</span> · gospel · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">εὐαγγέλιον</span></span> G2098 (76) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">εὐαγγελίζω</span></span> G2097 (54)</li>\n<li>grammar <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文法</span>: <span class=\"off\">Hiphil imperative <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使役命令</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">הָבֵ֥ן</span>, Dan 8:16)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">Hophal <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使役被動</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מֻעָ֣ף</span>, Dan 9:21)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">construct chain <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">附屬鏈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מַרְאֵה־ גָֽבֶר</span>, Dan 8:15)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 4 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"13-evaluation-評估\">12. Evaluation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">評估</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> the name marks the two great announcements. Gabriel explains the time of the end to Daniel and announces John and Jesus. </li>\n<li><strong>Where it is strongest:</strong> <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.15-19\">Dan 8:15–19</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.20-27\">9:20–27</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.11-20\">Luke 1:11–20</span>, 26–38 and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.14\">Heb 1:14</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>For whom:</strong> the congregation (the Lord's word comes by a sent messenger and is not void of power), new believers (what Gabriel is and is not), and teachers meeting the claims of tradition or of Islam about him.</li>\n<li><strong>Limits:</strong> four verses do not give an angelology. They do not rank him, number him or describe him. The meaning of the name and the sense of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">Dan 9:21</span> stay open, with the positions and sources stated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-sources-and-to-check-資料與待查\">13. Sources 資料 <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Texts and tags: BHSA (ETCBC), OpenGNT 3.3, the chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New with OpenBible.info verse votes for section 6, eBible KJV 1769 and ASV 1901 with Strong's tags, OpenHebrewBible and OpenGNT <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> alignments, UBS SDBH and UBS Greek NT dictionaries, ISBE (1915).</li>\n<li>Editions: 和合本; KJV 1769; ASV 1901; BHSA (Hebrew); OpenGNT (Greek NT); LXX = the Apostolic Bible Polyglot (the Sixtine of 1587 as reprinted by Bos 1709, not Rahlfs–Hanhart); Textus Receptus = Scrivener 1894 where it is named.</li>\n<li>The LXX used here (the Apostolic Bible Polyglot) is the Sixtine of 1587 via Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart; Daniel there is Theodotion. Every LXX line here (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.8.16\">Dan 8:16</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.21\">9:21</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.9.23\">23</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.10.11\">10:11</span>) is that text.</li>\n\n\n\n\n</ul>\n<details class=\"pn across\" open><summary>跨燈 <i lang=\"en\">Across the lamps</i></summary><p class=\"acr\" data-b=\"1\"></p></details>"}});
